WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.758
What System Design Is.

2
00:00:02.038 --> 00:00:06.646
Chapter 01, from the Orientation part of the System Design Playbook.

3
00:00:08.396 --> 00:00:09.377
At a glance:

4
00:00:09.657 --> 00:00:10.831
Chapter at a glance.

5
00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:12.135
Reader bands:

6
00:00:12.415 --> 00:00:14.719
B1, then B4, Prerequisites:

7
00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:17.324
Ch 00, What you will build:

8
00:00:17.604 --> 00:00:29.039
A pincode serviceability check, taken from an in-memory map (L1) to a multi-instance, event-refreshed service with an explicit consistency guarantee (L4), Reading time:

9
00:00:29.319 --> 00:00:30.791
90 min + code.

10
00:00:32.141 --> 00:00:32.994
Part 1.

11
00:00:33.274 --> 00:00:35.173
Introduction and Why It Matters.

12
00:00:36.423 --> 00:00:39.153
A story: the orders that could never be delivered.

13
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:44.767
A grocery platform added 40 new pincodes when it opened a dark store in a new suburb.

14
00:00:45.047 --> 00:00:48.801
Three weeks later, the store closed temporarily for a lease dispute.

15
00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:54.521
Operations disabled those pincodes in the admin panel at 11:02 am.

16
00:00:54.801 --> 00:00:58.940
Orders from those pincodes kept arriving until 3:40 pm.

17
00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:01.485
Nothing had crashed.

18
00:01:01.765 --> 00:01:03.215
Every service was green.

19
00:01:03.495 --> 00:01:10.791
The serviceability check was fast (2 ms p99) because it read from a cache that refreshed every 6 hours.

20
00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:14.186
Someone had chosen that number years earlier for performance.

21
00:01:14.466 --> 00:01:22.274
Over those four and a half hours, 1,900 orders were accepted, paid for through UPI, and could not be delivered.

22
00:01:22.554 --> 00:01:28.058
Customer support worked through the weekend and 1,900 refunds had to be issued.

23
00:01:28.338 --> 00:01:29.575
The code was correct.

24
00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:31.114
The design was wrong.

25
00:01:31.394 --> 00:01:36.023
No one had written down how stale a serviceability answer was allowed to be.

26
00:01:36.303 --> 00:01:43.770
A requirement that was never stated can't be met, so the system settled on whatever an engineer had once picked for convenience.

27
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:45.824
Quote:

28
00:01:46.104 --> 00:01:47.747
The thesis of this chapter.

29
00:01:48.027 --> 00:01:58.715
System design is the practice of making the important decisions about a system deliberately and explicitly: what it must do, how well, under what failures, and at what cost.

30
00:01:58.995 --> 00:02:01.363
It doesn't mean using the most technology.

31
00:02:02.713 --> 00:02:05.017
Where this lives in the grocery platform.

32
00:02:05.297 --> 00:02:09.798
Serviceability is on the critical path of almost every customer journey:

33
00:02:11.148 --> 00:02:12.471
Here is a quick table:

34
00:02:12.751 --> 00:02:16.313
Journey, and Serviceability question, and Cost of a wrong answer.

35
00:02:16.593 --> 00:02:17.532
App open.

36
00:02:17.812 --> 00:02:19.263
Serviceability question:

37
00:02:19.543 --> 00:02:21.377
Do we deliver to the saved address?

38
00:02:21.657 --> 00:02:22.980
Cost of a wrong answer:

39
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.244
Customer leaves (lost revenue).

40
00:02:25.524 --> 00:02:26.633
Address change.

41
00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:28.364
Serviceability question:

42
00:02:28.644 --> 00:02:30.351
Which store serves this pincode?

43
00:02:30.631 --> 00:02:31.953
Cost of a wrong answer:

44
00:02:32.233 --> 00:02:34.153
Wrong catalog and prices shown.

45
00:02:34.433 --> 00:02:35.308
Checkout.

46
00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:37.039
Serviceability question:

47
00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:39.004
Can this order still be delivered?

48
00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:40.607
Cost of a wrong answer:

49
00:02:40.887 --> 00:02:44.108
Undeliverable paid order (refund plus support cost).

50
00:02:44.388 --> 00:02:45.519
Slot selection.

51
00:02:45.799 --> 00:02:47.249
Serviceability question:

52
00:02:47.529 --> 00:02:49.193
Which slots are available here?

53
00:02:49.473 --> 00:02:50.796
Cost of a wrong answer:

54
00:02:51.076 --> 00:02:52.313
Overbooked riders.

55
00:02:53.663 --> 00:02:56.394
That's why this chapter's code ladder is built on it.

56
00:02:56.674 --> 00:03:03.714
The problem looks trivial (a map lookup), but its design depends on every quality attribute this chapter introduces.

57
00:03:05.064 --> 00:03:06.365
Learning objectives.

58
00:03:06.645 --> 00:03:07.968
Here is a quick table:

59
00:03:08.248 --> 00:03:10.488
Band, and After this chapter you can....

60
00:03:10.768 --> 00:03:11.600
B1:

61
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:20.093
Explain HLD versus LLD and functional versus non-functional requirements, and name the eight core quality attributes.

62
00:03:20.373 --> 00:03:21.248
B2:

63
00:03:21.528 --> 00:03:26.904
Turn vague requirements into measurable NFRs, and follow a repeatable design process.

64
00:03:27.184 --> 00:03:28.101
B3:

65
00:03:28.381 --> 00:03:35.101
Do availability math, find where quality attributes conflict, and design explicitly for staleness and failure.

66
00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:36.277
B4:

67
00:03:36.557 --> 00:03:49.507
Frame decisions as reversible versus irreversible, weigh cost and operability as first-class attributes, and place monolith or SOA or microservices or modular monolith in context.

68
00:03:50.857 --> 00:03:51.731
Part 2.

69
00:03:52.011 --> 00:03:53.910
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

70
00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.253
Intuition: designing a house versus building a wall.

71
00:03:58.533 --> 00:04:00.645
When you write code, you are laying bricks.

72
00:04:00.925 --> 00:04:10.312
When you design a system, you are the architect deciding how many floors, where the load-bearing walls go, where the water pipes run, and what happens in an earthquake.

73
00:04:10.592 --> 00:04:13.920
The architect decides three things before any bricks are laid:

74
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:15.459
What the house must do.

75
00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:17.296
Four bedrooms, a kitchen.

76
00:04:17.576 --> 00:04:19.304
These are functional requirements.

77
00:04:19.584 --> 00:04:20.885
How well it must do it.

78
00:04:21.165 --> 00:04:26.733
Survive a magnitude-6 earthquake, stay under 80 lakh rupees, be built in 10 months.

79
00:04:27.013 --> 00:04:29.083
These are non-functional requirements.

80
00:04:30.433 --> 00:04:31.905
How the parts fit together.

81
00:04:32.185 --> 00:04:34.574
Foundation, frame, plumbing, wiring.

82
00:04:34.854 --> 00:04:36.433
This is the architecture.

83
00:04:36.713 --> 00:04:43.049
A brilliant bricklayer can't save a house whose foundation was sized for two floors when the owner wanted five.

84
00:04:44.399 --> 00:04:46.191
HLD versus LLD.

85
00:04:46.471 --> 00:04:47.793
Here is a quick table:

86
00:04:48.073 --> 00:04:52.127
High-Level Design (HLD), and Low-Level Design (LLD).

87
00:04:52.407 --> 00:04:53.729
Question it answers.

88
00:04:54.009 --> 00:04:56.036
High-Level Design (HLD):

89
00:04:56.316 --> 00:04:58.556
What are the parts and how do they interact?

90
00:04:58.836 --> 00:05:00.756
Low-Level Design (LLD):

91
00:05:01.036 --> 00:05:02.785
How is each part built inside?

92
00:05:03.065 --> 00:05:04.047
Artifacts.

93
00:05:04.327 --> 00:05:06.353
High-Level Design (HLD):

94
00:05:06.633 --> 00:05:17.151
Component or C4 diagrams, data flow, service boundaries, storage choices, API contracts between services, sequence diagrams across services.

95
00:05:17.431 --> 00:05:19.351
Low-Level Design (LLD):

96
00:05:19.631 --> 00:05:28.036
Class diagrams, method signatures, DB schemas with indexes, algorithms, concurrency model, error handling within a service.

97
00:05:28.316 --> 00:05:29.489
Typical audience.

98
00:05:29.769 --> 00:05:31.796
High-Level Design (HLD):

99
00:05:32.076 --> 00:05:34.700
Architects, tech leads, product, SRE.

100
00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:36.900
Low-Level Design (LLD):

101
00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.271
Developers implementing the component.

102
00:05:40.621 --> 00:05:42.135
Serviceability example.

103
00:05:42.415 --> 00:05:55.770
High-Level Design (HLD): serviceability-service owns pincode, then store mappings in PostgreSQL, serves reads from an in-memory snapshot, and publishes serviceability.area.changed.v1.

104
00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:57.991
Checkout re-validates synchronously.

105
00:05:58.271 --> 00:06:00.191
Low-Level Design (LLD):

106
00:06:00.471 --> 00:06:08.343
ServiceabilitySnapshot is an immutable record held in an AtomicReference, refreshed by a scheduled job and by a Kafka listener.

107
00:06:08.623 --> 00:06:11.482
Lookup is MapString, SetStoreId, O(1).

108
00:06:11.762 --> 00:06:12.871
Interview format.

109
00:06:13.151 --> 00:06:15.178
High-Level Design (HLD):

110
00:06:15.458 --> 00:06:18.637
Design Swiggy Instamart (45 to 60 min).

111
00:06:18.917 --> 00:06:20.837
Low-Level Design (LLD):

112
00:06:21.117 --> 00:06:25.341
Design a parking lot or rate limiter class (45 min, code).

113
00:06:25.621 --> 00:06:26.645
Changes cost.

114
00:06:26.925 --> 00:06:28.951
High-Level Design (HLD):

115
00:06:29.231 --> 00:06:31.109
High: crosses teams and data.

116
00:06:31.389 --> 00:06:33.309
Low-Level Design (LLD):

117
00:06:33.589 --> 00:06:35.850
Lower: usually within one team's code.

118
00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:37.840
Tip:

119
00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:39.997
The boundary is fuzzy on purpose.

120
00:06:40.277 --> 00:06:51.157
A choice that looks like LLD, such as the snapshot refreshes every 6 hours, can have HLD consequences, like 1,900 undeliverable orders.

121
00:06:51.437 --> 00:06:59.032
Senior engineers recognize when a low-level parameter encodes a high-level requirement and pull it up into the design document.

122
00:07:00.382 --> 00:07:03.177
Functional versus Non-Functional Requirements.

123
00:07:03.457 --> 00:07:08.235
Functional requirements (FRs) describe behaviour, meaning what the system does.

124
00:07:08.515 --> 00:07:09.454
FR-1:

125
00:07:09.734 --> 00:07:14.747
Given a 6-digit pincode, return whether it is serviceable and which store(s) serve it.

126
00:07:15.027 --> 00:07:15.966
FR-2:

127
00:07:16.246 --> 00:07:19.915
Operations can enable or disable a pincode for a store.

128
00:07:20.195 --> 00:07:21.177
FR-3:

129
00:07:21.457 --> 00:07:25.105
Checkout must reject orders to non-serviceable pincodes.

130
00:07:25.385 --> 00:07:32.403
Non-functional requirements (NFRs) describe qualities, meaning how well the system does it and under what constraints.

131
00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:34.799
NFR-1:

132
00:07:35.079 --> 00:07:41.137
Lookup latency p99 at most 10 ms at the service, at most 50 ms end-to-end.

133
00:07:41.417 --> 00:07:42.441
NFR-2:

134
00:07:42.721 --> 00:07:46.583
Lookup availability 99.95 % monthly.

135
00:07:46.863 --> 00:07:47.951
NFR-3:

136
00:07:48.231 --> 00:07:53.607
A disable takes effect for checkout within 10 seconds and for browsing within 60 seconds.

137
00:07:53.887 --> 00:07:54.975
NFR-4:

138
00:07:55.255 --> 00:07:59.137
Must sustain 6,000 lookups per second at flash-sale peak.

139
00:07:59.417 --> 00:08:01.999
NFR-3 was the one missing in the story.

140
00:08:03.349 --> 00:08:06.442
Turning vague requirements into measurable NFRs.

141
00:08:06.722 --> 00:08:10.285
Stakeholders speak in adjectives, and designs need numbers.

142
00:08:10.565 --> 00:08:16.133
Use the SMART-NFR conversion: metric, percentile, threshold, scope, measurement window.

143
00:08:17.483 --> 00:08:18.805
Here is a quick table:

144
00:08:19.085 --> 00:08:22.072
Stakeholder says, and Ask, and Measurable NFR.

145
00:08:22.352 --> 00:08:23.483
It must be fast.

146
00:08:23.763 --> 00:08:24.424
Ask:

147
00:08:24.704 --> 00:08:26.880
Fast for whom, doing what, measured where?

148
00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:28.504
Measurable NFR:

149
00:08:28.784 --> 00:08:37.659
Product page API p99 at most 300 ms at the gateway, measured over 5-min windows, excluding client network.

150
00:08:37.939 --> 00:08:39.389
It must never go down.

151
00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:40.331
Ask:

152
00:08:40.611 --> 00:08:42.424
What does an hour of downtime cost?

153
00:08:42.704 --> 00:08:44.603
What's acceptable during a deploy?

154
00:08:44.883 --> 00:08:46.227
Measurable NFR:

155
00:08:46.507 --> 00:08:53.163
99.95 % monthly availability for checkout ( about 22 min per month budget).

156
00:08:53.443 --> 00:08:54.872
Data must be accurate.

157
00:08:55.152 --> 00:08:55.813
Ask:

158
00:08:56.093 --> 00:08:57.544
Accurate within how long?

159
00:08:57.824 --> 00:08:58.784
Which data?

160
00:08:59.064 --> 00:09:00.408
Measurable NFR:

161
00:09:00.688 --> 00:09:06.448
Stock shown on product page may lag at most 5 s; reservation at checkout must be exact.

162
00:09:06.728 --> 00:09:07.773
It must scale.

163
00:09:08.053 --> 00:09:08.715
Ask:

164
00:09:08.995 --> 00:09:10.125
To what, by when?

165
00:09:10.405 --> 00:09:11.749
Measurable NFR:

166
00:09:12.029 --> 00:09:18.600
3 times current peak (18 K req per second) by next Diwali with at most 20 % cost increase.

167
00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:21.145
It must be secure.

168
00:09:21.425 --> 00:09:22.086
Ask:

169
00:09:22.366 --> 00:09:23.561
Against which threats?

170
00:09:23.841 --> 00:09:25.099
Which regulations?

171
00:09:25.379 --> 00:09:26.723
Measurable NFR:

172
00:09:27.003 --> 00:09:37.435
OTP brute force limited to 5 attempts per 15 min per number; PII encrypted at rest; DPDP-compliant deletion within 30 days of request.

173
00:09:38.785 --> 00:09:39.532
Warning:

174
00:09:39.812 --> 00:09:40.985
Averages lie.

175
00:09:41.265 --> 00:09:42.951
Always ask for percentiles.

176
00:09:43.231 --> 00:09:52.617
A 50 ms average can hide a p99 of 2 s, and at 5,000 req per second that's 50 people per second with a bad experience.

177
00:09:53.967 --> 00:09:56.101
The Eight Core Quality Attributes, then.

178
00:09:56.381 --> 00:09:57.703
Here is a quick table:

179
00:09:57.983 --> 00:10:02.101
Attribute, and Plain meaning, and How it is measured, and Grocery example.

180
00:10:02.381 --> 00:10:03.426
Scalability.

181
00:10:03.706 --> 00:10:04.709
Plain meaning:

182
00:10:04.989 --> 00:10:07.271
Handles more load by adding resources.

183
00:10:07.551 --> 00:10:08.703
How it is measured:

184
00:10:08.983 --> 00:10:12.631
Throughput versus cost curve; max sustainable QPS.

185
00:10:12.911 --> 00:10:14.149
Grocery example:

186
00:10:14.429 --> 00:10:16.882
20 times traffic at a sale's first minute.

187
00:10:17.162 --> 00:10:18.229
Availability.

188
00:10:18.509 --> 00:10:19.511
Plain meaning:

189
00:10:19.791 --> 00:10:22.074
Is up and answering correctly when needed.

190
00:10:22.354 --> 00:10:26.557
How it is measured: % successful requests over a window (SLO).

191
00:10:26.837 --> 00:10:28.074
Grocery example:

192
00:10:28.354 --> 00:10:30.935
Checkout 99.95 %.

193
00:10:31.215 --> 00:10:32.815
Latency or Performance.

194
00:10:33.095 --> 00:10:34.098
Plain meaning:

195
00:10:34.378 --> 00:10:35.551
Responds quickly.

196
00:10:35.831 --> 00:10:42.871
How it is measured: p50 or p95 or p99 or p999 response time.

197
00:10:43.151 --> 00:10:44.389
Grocery example:

198
00:10:44.669 --> 00:10:47.677
Search p99 at most 200 ms.

199
00:10:47.957 --> 00:10:49.002
Consistency.

200
00:10:49.282 --> 00:10:50.285
Plain meaning:

201
00:10:50.565 --> 00:10:52.826
Everyone sees the same, correct data.

202
00:10:53.106 --> 00:10:54.258
How it is measured:

203
00:10:54.538 --> 00:10:56.778
Staleness bound; anomalies allowed.

204
00:10:57.058 --> 00:10:58.295
Grocery example:

205
00:10:58.575 --> 00:11:01.178
Inventory reservation must be exact.

206
00:11:01.458 --> 00:11:02.418
Durability.

207
00:11:02.698 --> 00:11:03.701
Plain meaning:

208
00:11:03.981 --> 00:11:06.455
Once acknowledged, data is never lost.

209
00:11:06.735 --> 00:11:07.887
How it is measured:

210
00:11:08.167 --> 00:11:10.151
RPO; probability of loss.

211
00:11:10.431 --> 00:11:11.669
Grocery example:

212
00:11:11.949 --> 00:11:13.911
Paid order must never disappear.

213
00:11:15.261 --> 00:11:15.987
Cost.

214
00:11:16.267 --> 00:11:17.269
Plain meaning:

215
00:11:17.549 --> 00:11:18.936
Money to build and run.

216
00:11:19.216 --> 00:11:23.355
How it is measured: ₹ per order; infra ₹/month; people.

217
00:11:23.635 --> 00:11:24.872
Grocery example:

218
00:11:25.152 --> 00:11:27.733
Infra at most 2.5 rupeesper order.

219
00:11:28.013 --> 00:11:29.059
Operability.

220
00:11:29.339 --> 00:11:30.341
Plain meaning:

221
00:11:30.621 --> 00:11:33.352
Easy to deploy, observe, debug, recover.

222
00:11:33.632 --> 00:11:34.784
How it is measured:

223
00:11:35.064 --> 00:11:38.051
MTTR; deploy frequency; toil hours.

224
00:11:38.331 --> 00:11:39.568
Grocery example:

225
00:11:39.848 --> 00:11:42.280
On-call can disable a store in one click.

226
00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:44.139
Security and Compliance.

227
00:11:44.419 --> 00:11:45.421
Plain meaning:

228
00:11:45.701 --> 00:11:47.963
Protected against misuse; lawful.

229
00:11:48.243 --> 00:11:49.395
How it is measured:

230
00:11:49.675 --> 00:11:52.064
Threat model coverage; audit findings.

231
00:11:52.344 --> 00:11:53.581
Grocery example:

232
00:11:53.861 --> 00:11:57.616
OTP abuse limits; DPDP; PCI scope.

233
00:11:58.966 --> 00:12:06.390
Other attributes you'll meet later: maintainability, testability, portability, extensibility, accessibility, privacy.

234
00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:10.254
They follow the same discipline: define it, measure it, trade it.

235
00:12:11.604 --> 00:12:12.649
Scalability.

236
00:12:12.929 --> 00:12:18.647
A system is scalable if adding resources adds proportional capacity without redesign.

237
00:12:18.927 --> 00:12:21.252
Vertical scaling means a bigger machine.

238
00:12:21.532 --> 00:12:29.169
It's simple and needs no code change, but it has a hard ceiling, is a single point of failure, and gets expensive nonlinearly.

239
00:12:29.449 --> 00:12:31.945
Horizontal scaling means more machines.

240
00:12:32.225 --> 00:12:40.737
It has no hard ceiling and gives redundancy, but it requires statelessness or partitioned state, plus load balancing and coordination.

241
00:12:42.087 --> 00:12:49.725
Scalability is always relative to a dimension: users, data volume, request rate, number of sellers, geography.

242
00:12:50.005 --> 00:12:54.698
A design that scales to 10 times users may fail at 10 times SKUs.

243
00:12:56.048 --> 00:12:58.224
Availability and the math of nines.

244
00:12:58.504 --> 00:12:59.485
The formula:

245
00:12:59.765 --> 00:13:07.872
Availability = (successful requests) / (valid requests) or (MTBF) / (MTBF + MTTR).

246
00:13:09.222 --> 00:13:10.545
Here is a quick table:

247
00:13:10.825 --> 00:13:14.665
SLO, and Downtime or month (30 d), and Downtime or year.

248
00:13:14.945 --> 00:13:16.203
99 %.

249
00:13:16.483 --> 00:13:18.446
Downtime or month (30 d):

250
00:13:18.726 --> 00:13:20.241
7 h 12 min.

251
00:13:20.521 --> 00:13:21.694
Downtime or year:

252
00:13:21.974 --> 00:13:23.617
3.65 days.

253
00:13:23.897 --> 00:13:25.689
99.9 %.

254
00:13:25.969 --> 00:13:27.931
Downtime or month (30 d):

255
00:13:28.211 --> 00:13:29.726
43.2 min.

256
00:13:30.006 --> 00:13:31.179
Downtime or year:

257
00:13:31.459 --> 00:13:33.038
8.76 h.

258
00:13:33.318 --> 00:13:35.387
99.95 %.

259
00:13:35.667 --> 00:13:37.630
Downtime or month (30 d):

260
00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:39.403
21.6 min.

261
00:13:39.683 --> 00:13:40.857
Downtime or year:

262
00:13:41.137 --> 00:13:42.523
4.38 h.

263
00:13:42.803 --> 00:13:44.830
99.99 %.

264
00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:47.073
Downtime or month (30 d):

265
00:13:47.353 --> 00:13:48.803
4.32 min.

266
00:13:49.083 --> 00:13:50.257
Downtime or year:

267
00:13:50.537 --> 00:13:52.030
52.6 min.

268
00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:54.571
99.999 %.

269
00:13:54.851 --> 00:13:56.814
Downtime or month (30 d):

270
00:13:57.094 --> 00:13:58.182
26 s.

271
00:13:58.462 --> 00:13:59.635
Downtime or year:

272
00:13:59.915 --> 00:14:01.387
5.26 min.

273
00:14:02.737 --> 00:14:04.679
Serial dependencies multiply.

274
00:14:04.959 --> 00:14:09.801
If checkout synchronously calls five services, each at 99.9 %:

275
00:14:11.151 --> 00:14:12.133
The formula:

276
00:14:12.413 --> 00:14:21.735
0.999^5 about 0.995 Rightarrow 99.5 % about 3.6 h per month down.

277
00:14:23.085 --> 00:14:25.923
Parallel redundancy compounds in your favour.

278
00:14:26.203 --> 00:14:29.723
Two independent replicas at 99.9 %:

279
00:14:31.073 --> 00:14:32.054
The formula:

280
00:14:32.334 --> 00:14:37.987
1 - (1 to 0.999)^2 = 0.999999.

281
00:14:39.337 --> 00:14:46.847
...but only if their failures really are independent, meaning no shared database, zone, deploy pipeline, or config push.

282
00:14:48.197 --> 00:14:49.093
Watch out:

283
00:14:49.373 --> 00:14:50.674
The practical lesson.

284
00:14:50.954 --> 00:14:55.221
Every synchronous dependency on the critical path costs you availability.

285
00:14:55.501 --> 00:15:01.623
That is the single biggest reason for caches, async events, and degrade instead of fail designs.

286
00:15:01.903 --> 00:15:04.335
You'll see it in L3 and L4 below.

287
00:15:05.685 --> 00:15:08.395
MTTR matters as much as MTBF.

288
00:15:08.675 --> 00:15:17.848
Going from 99.9 % to 99.95 % can come from failing half as often or from recovering twice as fast.

289
00:15:18.128 --> 00:15:23.163
The second is often far cheaper: better alerts, one-click rollback, feature flags.

290
00:15:24.513 --> 00:15:25.430
Latency.

291
00:15:25.710 --> 00:15:28.163
Latency is the time for one request.

292
00:15:28.443 --> 00:15:30.555
Throughput is requests per unit time.

293
00:15:30.835 --> 00:15:35.486
They are related through Little's Law: concurrency = throughput times latency.

294
00:15:35.766 --> 00:15:44.982
At 5,000 req per second and 40 ms latency you have 200 requests in flight at any moment, which sets your thread and connection needs.

295
00:15:45.262 --> 00:15:47.097
Tail latency amplification.

296
00:15:47.377 --> 00:16:00.454
If a page fans out to 20 services and each has a 1 % chance of being slow, the page is slow $1 to 0.99^20 \approx 18\%$ of the time.

297
00:16:01.804 --> 00:16:04.961
Reference numbers (full table in Ch 02):

298
00:16:05.241 --> 00:16:19.919
L1 cache ~1 ns, RAM ~100 ns, SSD random read ~100 µs, same-AZ round trip ~0.5 ms, Mumbai ↔ Singapore ~60 ms.

299
00:16:20.199 --> 00:16:24.572
That last row is why the L3 design below serves lookups from memory.

300
00:16:24.852 --> 00:16:30.249
100 ns versus 0.5 ms is a 5,000 times difference.

301
00:16:31.599 --> 00:16:32.645
Consistency.

302
00:16:32.925 --> 00:16:37.383
Consistency here means how fresh and agreed-upon the data a reader sees is.

303
00:16:37.663 --> 00:16:40.949
That's different from the C in ACID, which is about invariants.

304
00:16:41.229 --> 00:16:43.981
A quick spectrum (full treatment in Ch 12):

305
00:16:45.331 --> 00:16:46.653
Here is a quick table:

306
00:16:46.933 --> 00:16:49.429
Model, and Guarantee, and Grocery use.

307
00:16:49.709 --> 00:16:51.288
Strong or linearizable.

308
00:16:51.568 --> 00:16:52.528
Guarantee:

309
00:16:52.808 --> 00:16:54.643
Every read sees the latest write.

310
00:16:54.923 --> 00:16:55.925
Grocery use:

311
00:16:56.205 --> 00:16:58.403
Stock reservation, payment status.

312
00:16:58.683 --> 00:16:59.771
Read-your-writes.

313
00:17:00.051 --> 00:17:01.011
Guarantee:

314
00:17:01.291 --> 00:17:02.656
You see your own updates.

315
00:17:02.936 --> 00:17:03.939
Grocery use:

316
00:17:04.219 --> 00:17:06.821
Customer edits address, sees it immediately.

317
00:17:07.101 --> 00:17:08.317
Bounded staleness.

318
00:17:08.597 --> 00:17:09.557
Guarantee:

319
00:17:09.837 --> 00:17:11.416
Data is at most T old.

320
00:17:11.696 --> 00:17:12.699
Grocery use:

321
00:17:12.979 --> 00:17:15.624
Serviceability at most 60 s for browsing.

322
00:17:15.904 --> 00:17:16.800
Eventual.

323
00:17:17.080 --> 00:17:18.040
Guarantee:

324
00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:19.813
Converges eventually.

325
00:17:20.093 --> 00:17:21.096
Grocery use:

326
00:17:21.376 --> 00:17:23.360
Ratings count, recommendations.

327
00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:29.958
The key design move is to assign a consistency level per use case, not per system.

328
00:17:30.238 --> 00:17:36.062
In the opening story, one staleness level (6 h) was applied to both browsing and checkout.

329
00:17:37.412 --> 00:17:38.372
Durability.

330
00:17:38.652 --> 00:17:41.937
Durability means an acknowledged write survives crashes.

331
00:17:42.217 --> 00:17:51.988
It's measured by RPO (Recovery Point Objective: how much data you can lose) and implemented through replication, WAL, fsync policy, and backups.

332
00:17:53.338 --> 00:17:54.085
Note:

333
00:17:54.365 --> 00:17:56.541
Availability is not Durability.

334
00:17:56.821 --> 00:17:59.317
A system can be available but not durable.

335
00:17:59.597 --> 00:18:03.735
An in-memory cache happily answers and loses everything on restart.

336
00:18:04.015 --> 00:18:11.674
It can also be durable but not available: the database is intact but the primary is down and failover takes 90 s.

337
00:18:11.954 --> 00:18:13.213
Payments need both.

338
00:18:13.493 --> 00:18:15.605
Recommendations need neither strongly.

339
00:18:16.955 --> 00:18:17.680
Cost.

340
00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:21.331
Cost is an architectural attribute, not an afterthought.

341
00:18:21.611 --> 00:18:27.264
Track it as unit economics: ₹ infra per order, per active user, per GB served.

342
00:18:27.544 --> 00:18:32.643
Architectural choices dominate cost far more than micro-optimizations do.

343
00:18:32.923 --> 00:18:43.163
Examples: cross-AZ traffic charges, over-provisioned always-on clusters, 26 services times 3 replicas times 3 environments, and log volume.

344
00:18:44.513 --> 00:18:45.558
Operability.

345
00:18:45.838 --> 00:18:51.150
Can a tired on-call engineer at 3 am understand what's wrong and fix it safely?

346
00:18:51.430 --> 00:19:01.393
Operability covers observability (Ch 24), safe deploys and rollbacks (Ch 26), runbooks, feature flags, and admin controls.

347
00:19:01.673 --> 00:19:07.155
A design with no observable signal for its most important guarantee is not operable.

348
00:19:07.435 --> 00:19:11.041
In the story, there was no metric for age of serviceability data.

349
00:19:12.391 --> 00:19:13.969
Security and Compliance.

350
00:19:14.249 --> 00:19:27.945
This covers authN or authZ, data protection, abuse prevention, and regulatory obligations (DPDP Act, RBI data localization, PCI DSS, GST record retention).

351
00:19:28.225 --> 00:19:31.980
It's treated fully in Ch 23 and Ch 36.

352
00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:37.807
At the design stage, the minimum is a threat list and the data classification of what you store.

353
00:19:39.157 --> 00:19:41.909
Quality attributes conflict: trade-off thinking.

354
00:19:42.189 --> 00:19:43.959
You can't maximize everything.

355
00:19:44.239 --> 00:19:47.077
Every design is a position in a trade-off space.

356
00:19:48.427 --> 00:19:49.749
Here is a quick table:

357
00:19:50.029 --> 00:19:53.848
Improving..., and Usually costs..., and Serviceability example.

358
00:19:54.128 --> 00:19:55.728
Latency (via caching).

359
00:19:56.008 --> 00:19:57.224
Usually costs...:

360
00:19:57.504 --> 00:19:59.168
Consistency (staleness).

361
00:19:59.448 --> 00:20:00.963
Serviceability example:

362
00:20:01.243 --> 00:20:03.931
In-memory snapshot is fast but can be stale.

363
00:20:04.211 --> 00:20:05.896
Consistency (sync checks).

364
00:20:06.176 --> 00:20:07.392
Usually costs...:

365
00:20:07.672 --> 00:20:09.528
Latency and availability.

366
00:20:09.808 --> 00:20:11.323
Serviceability example:

367
00:20:11.603 --> 00:20:17.853
Checking the DB on every call means +2 ms and DB outage = serviceability outage.

368
00:20:18.133 --> 00:20:20.395
Availability (serve stale on failure).

369
00:20:20.675 --> 00:20:21.891
Usually costs...:

370
00:20:22.171 --> 00:20:23.771
Correctness in rare windows.

371
00:20:24.051 --> 00:20:25.565
Serviceability example:

372
00:20:25.845 --> 00:20:29.045
If refresh fails, keep answering from the old snapshot.

373
00:20:29.325 --> 00:20:31.373
Durability (sync replication).

374
00:20:31.653 --> 00:20:32.869
Usually costs...:

375
00:20:33.149 --> 00:20:34.237
Write latency.

376
00:20:34.517 --> 00:20:36.032
Serviceability example:

377
00:20:36.312 --> 00:20:38.787
Every admin change waits for a replica ack.

378
00:20:39.067 --> 00:20:41.371
Scalability (more services, sharding).

379
00:20:41.651 --> 00:20:42.867
Usually costs...:

380
00:20:43.147 --> 00:20:44.704
Cost and operability.

381
00:20:44.984 --> 00:20:46.499
Serviceability example:

382
00:20:46.779 --> 00:20:50.512
A separate serviceability service versus a table in catalog.

383
00:20:50.792 --> 00:20:52.221
Security (more checks).

384
00:20:52.501 --> 00:20:53.717
Usually costs...:

385
00:20:53.997 --> 00:20:55.213
Latency, UX.

386
00:20:55.493 --> 00:20:57.008
Serviceability example:

387
00:20:57.288 --> 00:21:00.915
OTP on every login versus trusted-device tokens.

388
00:21:02.265 --> 00:21:03.779
How to resolve conflicts:

389
00:21:04.059 --> 00:21:05.275
Split the use case.

390
00:21:05.555 --> 00:21:07.774
Browsing and checkout have different needs.

391
00:21:08.054 --> 00:21:13.003
Give browsing speed and bounded staleness, and give checkout a fresh re-validation.

392
00:21:13.283 --> 00:21:16.142
This one move resolves most apparent conflicts.

393
00:21:16.422 --> 00:21:18.534
Make the trade-off explicit and bounded.

394
00:21:18.814 --> 00:21:24.126
Instead of eventually consistent, say stale by at most 60 s, measured, alerted.

395
00:21:24.406 --> 00:21:25.515
Price the failure.

396
00:21:25.795 --> 00:21:34.798
1,900 undeliverable orders times (refund cost + support cost + churn) against the cost of a 10-second refresh.

397
00:21:35.078 --> 00:21:36.614
Numbers end arguments.

398
00:21:36.894 --> 00:21:40.883
Prefer reversible choices when uncertain (§2.7).

399
00:21:42.233 --> 00:21:43.897
The System Design Process.

400
00:21:44.177 --> 00:21:48.081
This is the process used in real design work and in interviews.

401
00:21:48.361 --> 00:21:51.327
Time boxes are for a 45-minute interview.

402
00:21:51.607 --> 00:21:53.249
At work, each step is days.

403
00:21:54.599 --> 00:21:55.943
Here is the diagram.

404
00:21:59.793 --> 00:22:01.116
Here is a quick table:

405
00:22:01.396 --> 00:22:04.959
Step, and Interview time, and Key output, and Common mistake.

406
