466
00:23:39.439 --> 00:23:40.399
Key output:

467
00:23:40.679 --> 00:23:42.407
Trade-offs, what you'd do next.

468
00:23:42.687 --> 00:23:43.754
Common mistake:

469
00:23:44.034 --> 00:23:45.826
Claiming the design is perfect.

470
00:23:47.176 --> 00:23:50.291
Decision types: reversible versus irreversible.

471
00:23:50.571 --> 00:23:53.152
Not every decision deserves the same rigour.

472
00:23:54.502 --> 00:23:55.825
Here is a quick table:

473
00:23:56.105 --> 00:23:58.366
Type, and Examples, and How to decide.

474
00:23:58.646 --> 00:24:00.459
Two-way door (cheap to reverse).

475
00:24:00.739 --> 00:24:01.678
Examples:

476
00:24:01.958 --> 00:24:07.803
Cache TTL, library choice inside a service, instance size, a feature flag default.

477
00:24:08.083 --> 00:24:09.150
How to decide:

478
00:24:09.430 --> 00:24:11.457
Decide quickly, measure, adjust.

479
00:24:11.737 --> 00:24:14.105
One-way door (expensive to reverse).

480
00:24:14.385 --> 00:24:15.323
Examples:

481
00:24:15.603 --> 00:24:26.654
Service or data boundaries, public API shape, event schemas others consume, primary database, partition key, tenancy model, cloud region for regulated data.

482
00:24:26.934 --> 00:24:28.001
How to decide:

483
00:24:28.281 --> 00:24:30.841
Design doc, alternatives, review, ADR.

484
00:24:32.191 --> 00:24:46.889
An architect's leverage comes from spotting which decisions are one-way doors, and from turning one-way doors into two-way doors where possible: versioned APIs, an anti-corruption layer, the strangler pattern, feature flags, dual-running.

485
00:24:48.239 --> 00:24:49.114
Example:

486
00:24:49.394 --> 00:24:50.482
Serviceability.

487
00:24:50.762 --> 00:24:53.002
The refresh interval is a two-way door.

488
00:24:53.282 --> 00:24:57.271
It should be a config property with a metric and an alert, not a constant.

489
00:24:57.551 --> 00:25:09.199
The decision checkout re-validates against the source of truth is closer to a one-way door, because it creates a runtime dependency and a latency budget other teams will build around.

490
00:25:09.479 --> 00:25:10.866
Put it in the ADR.

491
00:25:12.216 --> 00:25:13.261
How We Got Here:

492
00:25:13.541 --> 00:25:17.296
Monolith, then SOA, then Microservices, then What's Next.

493
00:25:17.576 --> 00:25:18.920
Here is the diagram.

494
00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:24.093
Here is a quick table:

495
00:25:24.373 --> 00:25:28.234
Style, and Why it appeared, and What went wrong, and What it still does well.

496
00:25:28.514 --> 00:25:29.367
Monolith.

497
00:25:29.647 --> 00:25:30.650
Why it appeared:

498
00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:32.957
Simplest way to build and deploy.

499
00:25:33.237 --> 00:25:34.218
What went wrong:

500
00:25:34.498 --> 00:25:39.426
Big-ball-of-mud coupling; one deploy for all teams; scaling everything together.

501
00:25:39.706 --> 00:25:40.943
What it still does well:

502
00:25:41.223 --> 00:25:44.850
Fast to build, easy to debug, local transactions, cheap.

503
00:25:45.130 --> 00:25:45.898
SOA.

504
00:25:46.178 --> 00:25:47.181
Why it appeared:

505
00:25:47.461 --> 00:25:50.533
Reuse and integration across enterprise systems.

506
00:25:50.813 --> 00:25:51.794
What went wrong:

507
00:25:52.074 --> 00:25:57.941
Central ESB became a bottleneck and a monolith of its own; heavy contracts; smart pipes.

508
00:25:58.221 --> 00:25:59.458
What it still does well:

509
00:25:59.738 --> 00:26:03.365
Explicit service contracts, integration of legacy systems.

510
00:26:03.645 --> 00:26:04.903
Microservices.

511
00:26:05.183 --> 00:26:06.186
Why it appeared:

512
00:26:06.466 --> 00:26:11.095
Independent deploys and scaling per team; cloud and containers made it cheap.

513
00:26:11.375 --> 00:26:12.357
What went wrong:

514
00:26:12.637 --> 00:26:20.295
Distributed monoliths; network failures everywhere; data consistency pain; operational cost; too-small services.

515
00:26:20.575 --> 00:26:21.813
What it still does well:

516
00:26:22.093 --> 00:26:26.935
Team autonomy at scale, independent scaling, fault isolation (if done right).

517
00:26:28.285 --> 00:26:29.629
Modular monolith.

518
00:26:29.909 --> 00:26:30.912
Why it appeared:

519
00:26:31.192 --> 00:26:34.008
Get module boundaries without network boundaries.

520
00:26:34.288 --> 00:26:35.269
What went wrong:

521
00:26:35.549 --> 00:26:38.152
Needs discipline to keep modules decoupled.

522
00:26:38.432 --> 00:26:39.669
What it still does well:

523
00:26:39.949 --> 00:26:43.747
Best default for small teams and new domains; easy to split later.

524
00:26:44.027 --> 00:26:44.944
Cell-based.

525
00:26:45.224 --> 00:26:46.227
Why it appeared:

526
00:26:46.507 --> 00:26:49.024
Limit blast radius at very large scale.

527
00:26:49.304 --> 00:26:50.285
What went wrong:

528
00:26:50.565 --> 00:26:52.507
Complex routing and operations.

529
00:26:52.787 --> 00:26:54.024
What it still does well:

530
00:26:54.304 --> 00:26:58.101
Failure isolation for huge platforms (Ch 34).

531
00:26:59.451 --> 00:27:00.091
Tip:

532
00:27:00.371 --> 00:27:01.715
The architect's summary.

533
00:27:01.995 --> 00:27:07.179
Choose service boundaries for team autonomy and independent change rates, not for fashion.

534
00:27:07.459 --> 00:27:14.670
A 26-service design is justified when multiple teams need to ship independently and scale parts differently.

535
00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:25.958
For a 6-person team building an MVP, the same domain model is better built as a modular monolith with the same bounded contexts, then extracted along those seams.

536
00:27:26.238 --> 00:27:34.537
We use 26 services in this book to teach distributed concepts, and Ch 16 revisits when that is and isn't the right call.

