615
00:31:36.190 --> 00:31:40.051
ADR-011 Serviceability consistency levels.

616
00:31:41.401 --> 00:31:42.041
1.

617
00:31:42.321 --> 00:31:43.729
Context and Problem.

618
00:31:44.009 --> 00:31:48.873
Serviceability answers are read on every app open, address change, and checkout.

619
00:31:49.153 --> 00:31:53.676
The current 6-hour cache caused undeliverable paid orders after a store closure.

620
00:31:53.956 --> 00:31:57.092
We need fast reads with explicit, bounded staleness.

621
00:31:57.372 --> 00:31:58.055
2.

622
00:31:58.335 --> 00:31:59.679
Goals or Non-Goals.

623
00:31:59.959 --> 00:32:07.468
Goals: fast lookups; per-use-case consistency; operator changes take effect predictably; observable staleness.

624
00:32:07.748 --> 00:32:16.559
Non-goals: polygon or geofence-level serviceability (future, Ch 47); slot capacity (owned by slot-service).

625
00:32:17.909 --> 00:32:18.634
3.

626
00:32:18.914 --> 00:32:19.895
Requirements.

627
00:32:21.245 --> 00:32:22.568
Here is a quick table:

628
00:32:22.848 --> 00:32:24.363
ID, and Requirement.

629
00:32:24.643 --> 00:32:25.581
FR-1:

630
00:32:25.861 --> 00:32:29.531
Lookup by pincode, then serviceable flag + store IDs.

631
00:32:29.811 --> 00:32:30.749
FR-2:

632
00:32:31.029 --> 00:32:35.360
Ops can activate or pause or retire an area; changes are audited.

633
00:32:35.640 --> 00:32:36.621
FR-3:

634
00:32:36.901 --> 00:32:39.440
Checkout rejects non-serviceable pincodes.

635
00:32:39.720 --> 00:32:40.765
NFR-1:

636
00:32:41.045 --> 00:32:44.736
Browse lookup p99 at most 10 ms in-service.

637
00:32:45.016 --> 00:32:46.040
NFR-2:

638
00:32:46.320 --> 00:32:51.376
Fresh (checkout) lookup p99 at most 50 ms, timeout 80 ms.

639
00:32:51.656 --> 00:32:52.744
NFR-3:

640
00:32:53.024 --> 00:32:58.315
Staleness: browse at most 60 s; checkout at most 10 s after operator change.

641
00:32:58.595 --> 00:32:59.683
NFR-4:

642
00:32:59.963 --> 00:33:06.576
Availability 99.95 % for reads; reads survive DB outage (stale-serving).

643
00:33:06.856 --> 00:33:07.923
NFR-5:

644
00:33:08.203 --> 00:33:10.912
Sustain 6,000 lookups per second at peak.

645
00:33:12.262 --> 00:33:12.966
4.

646
00:33:13.246 --> 00:33:14.526
Capacity estimates.

647
00:33:14.806 --> 00:33:15.659
Lookups:

648
00:33:15.939 --> 00:33:23.235
2 M DAU times 5 lookups per day = 10 M per day, then about 116 per second average.

649
00:33:23.515 --> 00:33:27.185
Evening peak 5 times, then ~600 per second.

650
00:33:27.465 --> 00:33:32.670
Flash sale 20 times in the first minute, then about 2,300 per second.

651
00:33:32.950 --> 00:33:35.958
Design target 6,000 per second for headroom.

652
00:33:36.238 --> 00:33:45.923
Data: ~8,000 serviceable pincodes times ~2 stores times ~150 bytes about 2.4 MB in memory.

653
00:33:46.203 --> 00:33:47.953
Trivially fits on every pod.

654
00:33:48.233 --> 00:33:49.619
Fresh (checkout) checks:

655
00:33:49.899 --> 00:34:01.505
400 K orders per day about 5 per second average; festival day 1.5 M orders with a 20 times first-minute spike about 350 per second.

656
00:34:01.785 --> 00:34:06.307
A primary-key lookup at that rate is negligible load for PostgreSQL.

657
00:34:07.657 --> 00:34:08.980
Writes: tens per day.

658
00:34:09.260 --> 00:34:11.265
Read:write is roughly 10⁶:

659
00:34:11.545 --> 00:34:12.185
1.

660
00:34:13.535 --> 00:34:14.474
Success:

661
00:34:14.754 --> 00:34:16.226
What the estimate tells us.

662
00:34:16.506 --> 00:34:19.023
The dataset is tiny and read-dominated.

663
00:34:19.303 --> 00:34:26.791
The right design is a full in-memory replica on every instance, not a distributed cache or a DB call per request.

664
00:34:27.071 --> 00:34:28.693
The hard part isn't scale.

665
00:34:28.973 --> 00:34:30.999
It's freshness and failure behaviour.

666
00:34:32.349 --> 00:34:33.117
5.

667
00:34:33.397 --> 00:34:34.592
Proposed design.

668
00:34:34.872 --> 00:34:35.917
Source of truth:

669
00:34:36.197 --> 00:34:38.800
PostgreSQL table serviceablearea.

670
00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:41.725
Every read pod holds an immutable snapshot.

671
00:34:42.005 --> 00:34:55.211
Refresh happens (a) on serviceability.area.changed.v1 events (published via transactional outbox), typically within 1 to 2 s, and (b) as a full reload every 30 s as a safety net.

672
00:34:55.491 --> 00:34:58.413
Requests carry consistency=browsefresh.

673
00:34:58.693 --> 00:35:00.485
Browse is served from the snapshot.

674
00:35:00.765 --> 00:35:08.573
Fresh (checkout only, about 350 per second peak) always does a primary-key read against PostgreSQL.

675
00:35:08.853 --> 00:35:14.784
The split works because the high-volume path tolerates staleness and the low-volume path needs correctness.

