Alert:

1027
00:55:26.149 --> 00:55:30.544
90 s for 2 min, then page. serviceability.snapshot.version.

1028
00:55:30.824 --> 00:55:31.507
Type:

1029
00:55:31.787 --> 00:55:32.896
Gauge per pod.

1030
00:55:33.176 --> 00:55:40.536
Alert: max minus min across pods 0 for 2 min, then ticket. serviceability.snapshot.reload.failures.

1031
00:55:40.816 --> 00:55:41.499
Type:

1032
00:55:41.779 --> 00:55:42.611
Counter.

1033
00:55:42.891 --> 00:55:48.928
Alert: rate 0 for 5 min, then ticket. serviceability.lookupconsistency,outcome.

1034
00:55:49.208 --> 00:55:49.891
Type:

1035
00:55:50.171 --> 00:55:50.939
Timer.

1036
00:55:51.219 --> 00:55:58.920
Alert: fresh p99 50 ms (SLO burn), then page. checkout.serviceability.decisiondecision,mode.

1037
00:55:59.200 --> 00:55:59.883
Type:

1038
00:56:00.163 --> 00:56:00.995
Counter.

1039
00:56:01.275 --> 00:56:02.192
Alert:

1040
00:56:02.472 --> 00:56:05.928
UNKNOWN 0.5 % of checkouts, then page.

1041
00:56:06.208 --> 00:56:09.259
Outbox backlog (count() where publishedat is null).

1042
00:56:09.539 --> 00:56:10.221
Type:

1043
00:56:10.501 --> 00:56:11.312
Gauge.

1044
00:56:11.592 --> 00:56:12.509
Alert:

1045
00:56:12.789 --> 00:56:15.861
100 rows or oldest 60 s, then page.

1046
00:56:17.211 --> 00:56:22.609
Log fields: correlationId, pincode, consistency, snapshotVersion, outcome.

1047
00:56:22.889 --> 00:56:28.691
Trace spans: serviceability.lookup (attr: consistency), db.select serviceablearea.

1048
00:56:28.971 --> 00:56:29.739
SLOs:

1049
00:56:30.019 --> 00:56:36.590
Browse availability 99.95 %, p99 at most 10 ms in-service.

1050
00:56:36.870 --> 00:56:42.289
Fresh availability 99.9 %, p99 at most 50 ms.

1051
00:56:42.569 --> 00:56:43.849
Staleness SLO:

1052
00:56:44.129 --> 00:56:48.929
99 % of minutes have max pod snapshot age at most 60 s.

1053
00:56:50.279 --> 00:56:50.919
Tip:

1054
00:56:51.199 --> 00:56:52.991
Staleness is an SLO too.

1055
00:56:53.271 --> 00:56:57.559
Teams write latency and availability SLOs and forget freshness.

1056
00:56:57.839 --> 00:57:00.591
If a guarantee isn't measured, it isn't being kept.

1057
00:57:01.941 --> 00:57:02.879
Security.

1058
00:57:03.159 --> 00:57:09.474
The public endpoint only offers browse consistency, so external callers can't force DB reads.

1059
00:57:09.754 --> 00:57:17.306
That closes a cheap DoS vector. /internal/ is blocked at the gateway and by a Kubernetes NetworkPolicy.

1060
00:57:17.586 --> 00:57:21.661
Service identity via mTLS comes in Ch 23.

1061
00:57:21.941 --> 00:57:24.543
The admin API requires an ops role.

1062
00:57:24.823 --> 00:57:28.322
Every change records updatedby and flows to audit-service.

1063
00:57:28.602 --> 00:57:34.362
Pincode is not PII on its own, but combined with a user ID it's location data.

1064
00:57:34.642 --> 00:57:37.138
Keep it out of public logs keyed by user.

1065
00:57:38.488 --> 00:57:40.131
Performance and tuning knobs.

1066
00:57:40.411 --> 00:57:41.733
Here is a quick table:

1067
00:57:42.013 --> 00:57:45.128
Knob, and Default, and Guidance. full-reload-interval.

1068
00:57:45.408 --> 00:57:46.240
Default:

1069
00:57:46.520 --> 00:57:47.437
30 s.

1070
00:57:47.717 --> 00:57:48.613
Guidance:

1071
00:57:48.893 --> 00:58:00.776
Lower bound on worst-case staleness when events fail; 30 s times 3 pods = 0.1 reload per second, negligible. jakarta.persistence.query.timeout.

1072
00:58:01.056 --> 00:58:01.888
Default:

1073
00:58:02.168 --> 00:58:03.235
80 ms.

1074
00:58:03.515 --> 00:58:04.411
Guidance:

1075
00:58:04.691 --> 00:58:09.683
Must be below the checkout client's read timeout, or the client gives up before the server does.

1076
00:58:09.963 --> 00:58:11.776
Hikari maximum-pool-size.

1077
00:58:12.056 --> 00:58:12.888
Default:

1078
00:58:13.168 --> 00:58:13.872
10.

1079
00:58:14.152 --> 00:58:15.048
Guidance:

1080
00:58:15.328 --> 00:58:21.920
Fresh about 350 per second times 2 ms about 0.7 concurrent (Little's Law).

1081
00:58:22.200 --> 00:58:24.440
10 is plenty. connection-timeout.

1082
00:58:24.720 --> 00:58:25.552
Default:

1083
00:58:25.832 --> 00:58:27.368
250 ms.

1084
00:58:27.648 --> 00:58:28.544
Guidance:

1085
00:58:28.824 --> 00:58:33.219
Fail fast; queueing behind a sick DB turns latency into an outage.

1086
00:58:33.499 --> 00:58:34.629
Virtual threads.

1087
00:58:34.909 --> 00:58:36.189
Default: on.

1088
00:58:36.469 --> 00:58:37.365
Guidance:

1089
00:58:37.645 --> 00:58:43.128
I or O-light service; removes thread-pool sizing as a concern (Ch 19).

1090
00:58:44.478 --> 00:58:45.203
Cost.

1091
00:58:45.483 --> 00:58:52.694
Three pods times 0.5 vCPU or 512 MB handle 6 K req per second.

1092
00:58:52.974 --> 00:58:56.622
The Kafka topic is 1 partition with a few KB per day.

1093
00:58:56.902 --> 00:59:04.326
The cost is dominated by the fixed platform overhead per service (CI, dashboards, on-call), not by compute.

1094
00:59:04.606 --> 00:59:08.681
That's a real argument in the B4 question should this be its own service?

1095
00:59:08.961 --> 00:59:09.985
(Q15).

1096
00:59:11.335 --> 00:59:12.273
Part 7.

1097
00:59:12.553 --> 00:59:14.260
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1098
00:59:15.510 --> 00:59:16.150
1.

1099
00:59:16.430 --> 00:59:17.945
The unstated NFR.

1100
00:59:18.225 --> 00:59:19.035
Symptom:

1101
00:59:19.315 --> 00:59:23.902
Behaviour determined by a constant someone picked (TTL = 6h).

1102
00:59:24.182 --> 00:59:24.929
Cause:

1103
00:59:25.209 --> 00:59:27.385
Requirements captured as features only.

1104
00:59:27.665 --> 00:59:28.369
Fix:

1105
00:59:28.649 --> 00:59:33.235
Every cache, timeout, and retry value traces back to a written NFR.

1106
00:59:34.585 --> 00:59:41.647
Here is the Java code. after: staleness is a named, configured, measured property tied to NFR-3.

1107
00:59:44.497 --> 00:59:45.179
2.

1108
00:59:45.459 --> 00:59:47.806
One consistency level for the whole system.

1109
00:59:48.086 --> 00:59:48.897
Symptom:

1110
00:59:49.177 --> 00:59:53.827
Either everything is slow (all strong) or correctness bugs appear (all cached).

1111
00:59:54.107 --> 00:59:54.811
Fix:

1112
00:59:55.091 --> 00:59:58.718
Assign consistency per use case (browse versus checkout).

1113
00:59:58.998 --> 00:59:59.723
3.

1114
01:00:00.003 --> 01:00:03.289
Designing for scale you don't have, and skipping estimation.

1115
01:00:03.569 --> 01:00:04.379
Symptom:

1116
01:00:04.659 --> 01:00:08.350
Redis cluster plus sharding for a 2.4 MB dataset.

1117
01:00:08.630 --> 01:00:09.334
Fix:

1118
01:00:09.614 --> 01:00:11.022
Do the estimate first.

1119
01:00:11.302 --> 01:00:15.867
The numbers in §4 showed memory beats every distributed option here.

1120
01:00:16.147 --> 01:00:16.851
4.

1121
01:00:17.131 --> 01:00:19.051
Averages instead of percentiles.

1122
01:00:19.331 --> 01:00:20.142
Symptom:

1123
01:00:20.422 --> 01:00:26.267
Average 40 ms, while the p99 is 1.2 s and checkout conversion drops.

1124
01:00:26.547 --> 01:00:27.251
Fix:

1125
01:00:27.531 --> 01:00:31.243
SLOs on p99 or p999.

1126
01:00:31.523 --> 01:00:35.235
Dashboards show percentiles and never average them across pods.

1127
01:00:36.585 --> 01:00:37.353
5.

1128
01:00:37.633 --> 01:00:39.831
Silent fail-open on a correctness check.

1129
01:00:41.181 --> 01:00:51.314
Here is the Java code. before: dependency failure silently means yes. after: explicit tri-state + explicit policy (ADR), measured.

1130
01:00:54.164 --> 01:00:57.471
Fail-open versus fail-closed is a business decision.

1131
01:00:57.751 --> 01:00:59.393
Make it deliberately, per check.

1132
01:00:59.673 --> 01:01:01.615
Fail open for recommendations.

1133
01:01:01.895 --> 01:01:04.433
Fail closed for payments and deliverability.

1134
01:01:04.713 --> 01:01:05.460
6.

1135
01:01:05.740 --> 01:01:06.657
Dual write.

1136
01:01:08.007 --> 01:01:10.077
Here is the Java code. before:

1137
01:01:10.357 --> 01:01:21.599
DB commit succeeds, Kafka send fails, then pods never learn about the change. after: outbox row in the same transaction; relay publishes at-least-once (L4).

1138
01:01:24.449 --> 01:01:25.196
7.

1139
01:01:25.476 --> 01:01:26.799
Serving before ready.

1140
01:01:27.079 --> 01:01:27.889
Symptom:

1141
01:01:28.169 --> 01:01:34.207
Right after a deploy, new pods answer not serviceable for everything because the snapshot is empty.

1142
01:01:34.487 --> 01:01:35.191
Fix:

1143
01:01:35.471 --> 01:01:37.924
Readiness depends on the first successful load.

1144
01:01:38.204 --> 01:01:41.361
Never default an empty dataset to a business answer.

1145
01:01:41.641 --> 01:01:42.324
8.

1146
01:01:42.604 --> 01:01:44.353
Microservices by default.

1147
01:01:44.633 --> 01:01:45.444
Symptom:

1148
01:01:45.724 --> 01:01:50.972
A 5-person team runs 26 services, and every feature touches six repos.

1149
01:01:51.252 --> 01:01:51.956
Fix:

1150
01:01:52.236 --> 01:01:54.007
Draw bounded contexts first.

1151
01:01:54.287 --> 01:02:02.116
Deploy them as a modular monolith until team or scaling needs justify a split (§2.8, Q15).

1152
01:02:03.466 --> 01:02:04.319
Part 8.

1153
01:02:04.599 --> 01:02:05.922
Interview Q and A.

1154
01:02:07.172 --> 01:02:08.025
Beginner.

1155
01:02:08.305 --> 01:02:09.351
Question 1.

1156
01:02:09.631 --> 01:02:13.556
What is system design, and how is HLD different from LLD?

1157
01:02:13.836 --> 01:02:15.159
Thirty-second answer:

1158
01:02:15.439 --> 01:02:28.879
System design is deciding how a software system's parts fit together so that it meets both its functional requirements and its quality requirements (scale, availability, latency, cost) under real-world failures.

1159
01:02:29.159 --> 01:02:35.537
HLD covers the parts and their interactions: services, datastores, APIs, and data flow.

1160
01:02:35.817 --> 01:02:41.855
LLD covers how each part is built inside: classes, schemas, algorithms, concurrency.

1161
01:02:43.205 --> 01:02:44.207
Question 2.

1162
01:02:44.487 --> 01:02:48.199
What's the difference between functional and non-functional requirements?

1163
01:02:48.479 --> 01:02:49.567
Give examples.

1164
01:02:49.847 --> 01:02:51.170
Thirty-second answer:

1165
01:02:51.450 --> 01:02:57.125
Functional requirements say what the system does (reject orders to non-serviceable pincodes).

1166
01:02:57.405 --> 01:03:09.970
Non-functional requirements say how well it does it, as measurable targets (p99 at most 50 ms, 99.95 % availability, changes effective within 10 s).

1167
01:03:11.320 --> 01:03:12.365
Question 3.

1168
01:03:12.645 --> 01:03:13.925
What is scalability?

1169
01:03:14.205 --> 01:03:15.912
Vertical versus horizontal?

1170
01:03:16.192 --> 01:03:17.515
Thirty-second answer:

1171
01:03:17.795 --> 01:03:22.829
Scalability is handling more load by adding resources, ideally proportionally.

1172
01:03:23.109 --> 01:03:28.613
Vertical means a bigger machine: simple, but it has a ceiling and is a single point of failure.

1173
01:03:28.893 --> 01:03:38.173
Horizontal means more machines: no hard ceiling and built-in redundancy, but it needs stateless services or partitioned state, plus load balancing.

1174
01:03:39.523 --> 01:03:40.590
Question 4.

1175
01:03:40.870 --> 01:03:43.366
What's the difference between latency and throughput?

1176
01:03:43.646 --> 01:03:44.969
Thirty-second answer:

1177
01:03:45.249 --> 01:03:47.723
Latency is how long one request takes.

1178
01:03:48.003 --> 01:03:51.353
Throughput is how many requests are completed per unit time.

1179
01:03:51.633 --> 01:03:55.878
They're linked by Little's Law: concurrency = throughput times latency.

1180
01:03:57.228 --> 01:03:58.231
Intermediate.

1181
01:03:58.511 --> 01:03:59.620
Question 5.

1182
01:03:59.900 --> 01:04:04.465
Walk me through how you approach a system design problem in 45 minutes.

1183
01:04:04.745 --> 01:04:06.068
Thirty-second answer:

1184
01:04:06.348 --> 01:04:25.228
Clarify requirements and non-goals (5 min), estimate load and data (5), define APIs (5), data model and storage choice (5), draw the simplest working HLD (10), deep-dive the 2 to 3 hardest parts (10 to 15), then cover failure modes and trade-offs (5).

1185
01:04:26.578 --> 01:04:27.687
Question 6.

1186
01:04:27.967 --> 01:04:33.109
Checkout synchronously calls 5 services, each 99.9 % available.

1187
01:04:33.389 --> 01:04:36.311
What's checkout's availability, and how would you improve it?

1188
01:04:36.591 --> 01:04:37.914
Thirty-second answer:

1189
01:04:38.194 --> 01:04:47.602
About 0.999⁵ about 99.5 %, roughly 3.6 hours down per month, assuming independent failures.

1190
01:04:47.882 --> 01:05:00.853
Improve it by removing synchronous dependencies from the critical path (cache, async, precompute), adding redundancy, degrading instead of failing for non-critical calls, and reducing MTTR.

1191
01:05:02.203 --> 01:05:03.291
Question 7.

1192
01:05:03.571 --> 01:05:09.992
Give a grocery example where you'd choose strong consistency and one where you'd accept eventual consistency.

1193
01:05:10.272 --> 01:05:11.595
Thirty-second answer:

1194
01:05:11.875 --> 01:05:18.509
Strong for stock reservation and payment status, where a wrong answer means overselling or double charging.

1195
01:05:18.789 --> 01:05:26.469
Eventual (bounded) for product ratings, recommendations, and serviceability during browsing, where brief staleness costs little.

1196
01:05:27.819 --> 01:05:28.843
Question 8.

1197
01:05:29.123 --> 01:05:32.409
Why aren't microservices always better than a monolith?

1198
01:05:32.689 --> 01:05:34.011
Thirty-second answer:

1199
01:05:34.291 --> 01:05:44.787
Microservices trade in-process calls and local transactions for network calls, partial failures, distributed data consistency, and much higher operational cost.

1200
01:05:45.067 --> 01:05:50.145
They pay off for team autonomy and independent scaling at organizational scale.

1201
01:05:50.425 --> 01:05:54.307
They cost more than they return for small teams or unclear domains.

1202
01:05:55.657 --> 01:05:56.724
Question 9.

1203
01:05:57.004 --> 01:06:01.697
A product manager says 'the app must be fast.' How do you turn that into a requirement?

1204
01:06:01.977 --> 01:06:03.300
Thirty-second answer:

1205
01:06:03.580 --> 01:06:10.108
Ask fast for which journey, measured where, at what percentile, under what load, and what a slow experience costs.

1206
01:06:10.388 --> 01:06:11.753
Then propose numbers:

1207
01:06:12.033 --> 01:06:18.668
Home page API p99 at most 300 ms at the gateway at 3 times current peak.

1208
01:06:20.018 --> 01:06:20.786
Senior.

1209
01:06:21.066 --> 01:06:22.111
Question 10.

1210
01:06:22.391 --> 01:06:26.871
Our service meets its p99 SLO, but users say the app is slow.

1211
01:06:27.151 --> 01:06:28.239
What's going on?

1212
01:06:28.519 --> 01:06:29.842
Thirty-second answer:

1213
01:06:30.122 --> 01:06:50.111
Likely causes: the SLO is measured in the wrong place (service, not user), the percentile is too low for a fan-out page, averaging across pods hides hot pods, client-side or network time is excluded, or specific segments (a city, low-end devices, one API) are slow while the aggregate looks fine.

1214
01:06:51.461 --> 01:06:52.592
Question 11.

1215
01:06:52.872 --> 01:06:57.992
Which quality attributes typically conflict, and how do you resolve the conflict in practice?

1216
01:06:58.272 --> 01:06:59.595
Thirty-second answer:

1217
01:06:59.875 --> 01:07:15.469
Latency versus consistency (caching), availability versus consistency (partitions), durability versus write latency (sync replication), scalability versus operability and cost (more components), security versus UX.

1218
01:07:15.749 --> 01:07:23.813
I resolve them by splitting use cases, bounding the trade-off with numbers, pricing the failure, and preferring reversible choices.

1219
01:07:25.163 --> 01:07:26.251
Question 12.

1220
01:07:26.531 --> 01:07:32.121
Explain durability versus availability with an example where you have one but not the other.

1221
01:07:32.401 --> 01:07:33.723
Thirty-second answer:

1222
01:07:34.003 --> 01:07:36.777
Durability means acknowledged data isn't lost.

1223
01:07:37.057 --> 01:07:40.726
Availability means the system responds correctly when asked.

1224
01:07:41.006 --> 01:07:43.907
An in-memory cache is available but not durable.

1225
01:07:44.187 --> 01:07:51.910
A database whose primary is down while its data is safe on disk and replicas is durable but temporarily unavailable.

1226
01:07:53.260 --> 01:07:54.540
Question 13.

1227
01:07:54.820 --> 01:07:56.825
How do you design for operability?

1228
01:07:57.105 --> 01:07:58.428
Thirty-second answer:

1229
01:07:58.708 --> 01:08:16.095
Make the system observable (metrics for its key guarantees, not just CPU), safe to change (feature flags, canaries, fast rollback), safe to operate (admin controls with guardrails and audit), and quick to recover (runbooks, readiness and liveness done right, stale-serving modes).

1230
01:08:17.445 --> 01:08:18.639
Question 14.

1231
01:08:18.919 --> 01:08:23.890
A cached serviceability answer caused 1,900 undeliverable orders.

1232
01:08:24.170 --> 01:08:25.471
Walk me through your fix.

1233
01:08:25.751 --> 01:08:27.074
Thirty-second answer:

1234
01:08:27.354 --> 01:08:32.303
Immediately, shorten the refresh and make checkout re-validate against the source of truth.

1235
01:08:32.583 --> 01:08:47.538
Structurally, split consistency by use case (browse from snapshot with bounded staleness, checkout fresh and fail-closed), add event-driven refresh via outbox, make staleness a measured SLO, and roll out in shadow mode first.

1236
01:08:48.888 --> 01:08:49.805
Architect.

1237
01:08:50.085 --> 01:08:51.259
Question 15.

1238
01:08:51.539 --> 01:08:56.765
A team proposes 26 microservices for an MVP with 8 engineers.

1239
01:08:57.045 --> 01:08:59.221
What's your response in the design review?

1240
01:08:59.501 --> 01:09:00.824
Thirty-second answer:

1241
01:09:01.104 --> 01:09:14.459
Keep the 26 bounded contexts as the domain model, but deploy them as a modular monolith (or 3 to 4 deployables) until team count, scaling needs, or change-rate differences justify splitting.

1242
01:09:14.739 --> 01:09:18.429
Enforce module boundaries in code so extraction stays cheap.

1243
01:09:19.779 --> 01:09:20.974
Question 16.

1244
01:09:21.254 --> 01:09:22.897
You're starting a new platform.

1245
01:09:23.177 --> 01:09:25.545
How do you decide which NFRs matter most?

1246
01:09:25.825 --> 01:09:27.147
Thirty-second answer:

1247
01:09:27.427 --> 01:09:29.305
Start from business risk and value.

1248
01:09:29.585 --> 01:09:33.275
What failure is most expensive (money, trust, regulation)?

1249
01:09:33.555 --> 01:09:35.667
What quality differentiates the product?

1250
01:09:35.947 --> 01:09:42.945
Rank NFRs by those, set targets per user journey, and accept weaker targets elsewhere to fund the strong ones.

1251
01:09:44.295 --> 01:09:45.596
Question 17.

1252
01:09:45.876 --> 01:09:48.863
How do you treat cost as an architectural attribute?

1253
01:09:49.143 --> 01:09:50.465
Thirty-second answer:

1254
01:09:50.745 --> 01:10:01.860
Define unit economics (₹ infra per order or per active user), estimate them in the design doc, attribute costs to services and teams, and review them like latency.

1255
01:10:02.140 --> 01:10:09.500
Architecture decisions such as service count, replication factor, cross-AZ traffic, and retention dominate spend.

1256
01:10:10.850 --> 01:10:12.023
Question 18.

1257
01:10:12.303 --> 01:10:15.269
What are reversible versus irreversible decisions?

1258
01:10:15.549 --> 01:10:17.767
Give examples, and how you handle each.

1259
01:10:18.047 --> 01:10:19.370
Thirty-second answer:

1260
01:10:19.650 --> 01:10:28.034
Reversible (two-way door) decisions are cheap to undo: cache TTLs, library choices inside a service, instance sizes.

1261
01:10:28.314 --> 01:10:41.413
Irreversible (one-way door) decisions are expensive to undo: service and data boundaries, public APIs, event schemas, partition keys, primary database, tenancy model, data residency.

1262
01:10:41.693 --> 01:10:44.061
Decide reversible ones fast and measure.

1263
01:10:44.341 --> 01:10:49.141
Give irreversible ones design docs, alternatives, reviews, and ADRs.

1264
01:10:50.491 --> 01:10:51.728
Question 19.

1265
01:10:52.008 --> 01:10:54.931
How do you evaluate a design document you didn't write?

1266
01:10:55.211 --> 01:10:56.533
Thirty-second answer:

1267
01:10:56.813 --> 01:11:00.461
Check that requirements and NFRs are explicit and numeric.

1268
01:11:00.741 --> 01:11:03.557
Check that the estimate supports the chosen design.

1269
01:11:03.837 --> 01:11:14.803
Look for real alternatives, failure modes with detection and mitigation, operability, security, migration and rollback plans, cost, and one-way-door decisions called out.

1270
01:11:15.083 --> 01:11:16.939
Then probe the riskiest assumption.

1271
01:11:18.289 --> 01:11:19.419
Question 20.

1272
01:11:19.699 --> 01:11:23.774
Trace the evolution from monolith to SOA to microservices.

1273
01:11:24.054 --> 01:11:26.465
What's next, and what did each era teach us?

1274
01:11:26.745 --> 01:11:28.067
Thirty-second answer:

1275
01:11:28.347 --> 01:11:30.331
Monoliths were simple but coupled.

1276
01:11:30.611 --> 01:11:35.134
SOA added service contracts but centralized logic in ESBs.

1277
01:11:35.414 --> 01:11:44.118
Microservices gave team autonomy via small, independently deployed services with their own data, but brought distributed-systems cost.

1278
01:11:44.398 --> 01:11:54.339
The current correction is right-sized services, modular monoliths, platform engineering, and cell-based architectures for blast-radius control at huge scale.

1279
01:11:55.689 --> 01:11:56.585
Part 9.

1280
01:11:56.865 --> 01:11:58.273
Summary and Practice.

1281
01:11:59.523 --> 01:12:00.633
Key takeaways.

1282
01:12:00.913 --> 01:12:08.145
System design means making important decisions explicitly: requirements, qualities, failure behaviour, and cost.

1283
01:12:08.425 --> 01:12:10.963
HLD decides parts and interactions.

1284
01:12:11.243 --> 01:12:12.950
LLD decides internals.

1285
01:12:13.230 --> 01:12:16.643
A low-level parameter can encode a high-level requirement.

1286
01:12:16.923 --> 01:12:21.595
NFRs need metric + percentile + threshold + scope + window.

1287
01:12:21.875 --> 01:12:23.625
Fast is not a requirement.

1288
01:12:23.905 --> 01:12:33.270
The eight core attributes are scalability, availability, latency, consistency, durability, cost, operability, and security or compliance.

1289
01:12:33.550 --> 01:12:37.305
Freshness is part of consistency, and it needs an SLO too.

1290
01:12:38.655 --> 01:12:41.748
Serial dependencies multiply unavailability.

1291
01:12:42.028 --> 01:12:45.271
Redundancy helps only when failures are independent.

1292
01:12:45.551 --> 01:12:48.452
Choose consistency per use case, not per system.

1293
01:12:48.732 --> 01:12:50.375
Bound eventual with a number.

1294
01:12:50.655 --> 01:12:57.908
Resolve conflicts by splitting use cases, bounding trade-offs, pricing failures, and preferring reversible choices.

1295
01:12:58.188 --> 01:13:07.041
Follow the process: requirements, then estimate, then API, then data, then HLD, then deep dives, then failures, then trade-offs.

1296
01:13:07.321 --> 01:13:11.396
Spot one-way doors, and convert them to two-way doors where you can.

1297
01:13:12.746 --> 01:13:18.250
Choose monolith versus microservices by team autonomy and change rates, not fashion.

1298
01:13:19.600 --> 01:13:20.923
Decision cheat sheet.

1299
01:13:21.203 --> 01:13:22.525
Here is a quick table:

1300
01:13:22.805 --> 01:13:24.256
If..., and Then consider....

1301
01:13:24.536 --> 01:13:26.563
Dataset is small and read-mostly:

1302
01:13:26.843 --> 01:13:29.893
Full in-memory replica per instance + refresh.

1303
01:13:30.173 --> 01:13:32.648
Correctness matters on a low-volume path:

1304
01:13:32.928 --> 01:13:35.040
Read source of truth; fail closed.

1305
01:13:35.320 --> 01:13:37.432
High-volume path tolerates staleness:

1306
01:13:37.712 --> 01:13:40.507
Cache or snapshot with measured, bounded staleness.

1307
01:13:40.787 --> 01:13:43.069
Many consumers must react to a change:

1308
01:13:43.349 --> 01:13:45.056
Outbox + event topic.

1309
01:13:45.336 --> 01:13:47.107
Dependency is nice-to-have:

1310
01:13:47.387 --> 01:13:50.117
Timeout + fallback (fail open), metered.

1311
01:13:50.397 --> 01:13:52.189
Decision is hard to undo:

1312
01:13:52.469 --> 01:13:54.923
Design doc + ADR + review.

1313
01:13:55.203 --> 01:13:56.568
Small team, new domain:

1314
01:13:56.848 --> 01:13:59.408
Modular monolith with bounded contexts.

